nodecode-chmod-ref is a rewrite of olric3015's olric3015/hermes-plugins:

  https://github.com/olric3015/hermes-plugins
  commit d2f543a0979eee73202a3fe06bb2c722b04d5765
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 olric3015 (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the plugin's chmod-ref folder (modes.py, __init__.py) as
Common Lisp. modes.py is the whole of it -- the octal and ls -l readers, the
symbolic change applied the way GNU chmod applies it with a umask of 0, the
umask answer, the notes about setuid, setgid, sticky, classes and git, and
the wording of every line -- and MODES.LISP is that engine, worked out by the
same arithmetic and saying the same things, with no dependency beyond Common
Lisp. Every answer a user got from the plugin, this cell gives.

What was left out: the plugin registered a CONTEXT REFERENCE provider, a
Hermes-only input feature: a user wrote @chmod:755 inside a message and
Hermes replaced the reference with the explanation before the model saw it.
Nodecode has no such reference syntax, so the same engine is reached three
ways instead -- the tool chmod_explain for the model, /chmod for the
operator, and (nodecode-chmod-ref:explain "755") through eval -- and the
plugin's autocomplete suggestions (preview) became the one-line summaries
/chmod answers: not the completion menu, which Nodecode draws itself.

The last note a change's answer carried pointed at the reference syntax
(give a starting mode to see one result: @chmod:"644 u+x") and now names
the value itself (give a starting mode to see one result: 644 u+x).
